{
  "dom_id": "step-q51272",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/597798661d1596394974746dff36b9a6e943981ba220b5f71c7b3b19d4530333.png)\n\nFind the shaded area that lies between the polar curves $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the values of $\\theta $ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226762/assets/f75b0cf6fe65851a6b842a67e7753ae8f91c2588b57ac7ac973a50dae8bcc865.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$\\,$ ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}((4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}12{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3(\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2}){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2})] \\\\  & =3\\pi \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
